{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790987386,"nonce":"+rnF2hFliVRcZm3TJOmym25Y9OQ=","title":"the desktop test cannot drive input under GNOME on Wayland","message":"crates/xwindow-check/tests/desktop.rs drives Wayland input only under sway, through wlroots' virtual pointer and keyboard protocols, which mutter does not implement. Under GNOME Shell on Wayland it reports \"not under sway (no SWAYSOCK)\" and fails the five input checks.\n\nThe cursor grab and fullscreen checks also fail there: they wait for Focused(true) on the first window. GNOME does not focus a new Wayland window without user input or an activation token, and a session with no input gives neither. Without a driver those checks should skip, as focus does, rather than fail.\n\nMutter can drive input: org.gnome.Mutter.RemoteDesktop takes absolute pointer motion over a ScreenCast stream, buttons, axis steps and evdev keycodes, from any client on the session bus. A GNOME driver for the test would use it, and would find each window's place on the monitor by moving the pointer and reading CursorMoved, since a Wayland client cannot read its position.\n\nSeen 2026-10-03 on a NUC (Ubuntu 26.04, GNOME Shell 50.1, headless with one virtual monitor): Wayland 7 passed, 4 skipped, 7 failed; the same session under Xwayland, with xdotool, passed 17 and skipped 1.","files":null}]}